{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

The A/B Compression widget draws the plan of
[`get_ab_compression`](/docs/tools/get-ab-compression) step by step: it computes
nothing of its own. Its headless twin, `ab_compression_data`, is an alias of
`get_ab_compression` (same arguments, same frame), and every number in the
widget is a row of the plan or of its `attrs["path"]`.

### The path chart

One point per step of the greedy path, the vocabulary size on x (the full
vocabulary on the right; the path moves left as events are merged, dropped or
relabelled). Four panels share that axis, one measure each, so no panel needs a
second scale:

- **Testable cells**: the cells of the screen that pass the support rule;
- **Observations too thin to test**: the share of the screen's observations
  (each path's incidence on a cell) in cells below `min_support`, the measure
  the default `target` reads;
- **Median MDE** of the testable cells, at the family's `alpha / m`;
- **Transition mass retained**: the share of the original transitions that are
  still transitions after compression, not artificial ones.

With `target_delta`, a fifth panel shows the **users needed**, as a multiple of
the current users on a log scale (the dotted line is the data as it is): the
solid series is the multiple at which half of the step's testable cells detect
`target_delta`, the dashed one the multiple at which 90 % of its cells pass the
support rule. A legend names both.

A step that creates artificial transitions (compressed edges no original direct
transition maps to) is marked with a red ring, and the chosen step (by
`target`, or `step`) with a dark one. Each panel's range is printed beside it.

### The slider and the mapping table

The slider picks a step; it starts at the chosen one. It moves in the browser
without a recompute, in a notebook and in a static export alike: the payload
carries every step's mapping. At the selected step, the mapping table lists the
events whose label changed (or every event): the new label, dropped or `other`,
the step that set it, its share of the paths and the reason in words, the same
`reason` as the plan's. Before and after compares step 0 with the selected step:
vocabulary, cells, testable and untestable cells, the observations too thin to
test, median MDE, transition mass retained and artificial transitions (and, with
`target_delta`, the users needed).

The header names the screen, the population, the chosen step and the arm sizes,
and says that the plan is arm-blind: it reads the pooled paths, never which arm
a path is in. With `by`, a selector switches between the segments' plans, and
the header shows the segment's homogeneity (effective vocabulary, transition
entropy, distinct-path share).

To take the plan at the step shown:

```python
plan = stream.ab_compression_data(diff, assignment=assignment, step=3)
compact = stream.compress_events(plan)
```

### Sidebar and view

The sidebar edits `screen`, `min_path_share`, `chain_threshold`, `max_events`,
`target` ("chosen step by": the least data too thin to test, the most testable
cells or the lowest median MDE) and `target_delta` (empty: no users-needed
panel), and every change recomputes the plan; the other arguments are set in
code and shown read-only. Switching the screen sets aside the other screen's
argument (`edge_weight` or `max_steps`). The selected step, the "changed only"
filter and the segment are the view. A `state_file` keeps the data arguments and
the view; `diff` and `assignment` are passed again when the widget is reopened. A
static export keeps the slider and the view, and locks the data controls.

