{% block demo_examples %}
## Examples

The examples run on the bundled synthetic experiment, `load_ab_test()`, with its
assignment table, `load_ab_test_assignment()`, as `assignment`.

### A capped vocabulary for the transition screen

<DemoWidget cmd={`stream.ab_compression(
    ("exp_group", "treatment", "control"),
    assignment=assignment,
    keep=["quick_add", "cart"],
    max_events=10,
)`} path="/docs-demos/widgets/ab-compression/transitions-capped.html" height={640} />

The stream is dense (90 of 92 edges testable; its thin edges hold less than
0.01 % of the observations), so the default plan keeps it as it is; the cap of
10 events merges the checkout chain, `checkout_start+payment+order_success`,
and three rare detours with their closest neighbour, `home`, into
`reviews_view+wishlist_add+support_chat+home`, and drops nothing. 60 of the 61
edges of the compressed screen are testable, at a cost the chart shows: 5 % of
the transitions merged into longer runs, and a median MDE of 2.55 pp instead of
2.03.

### The step screen's plan

<DemoWidget cmd={`stream.ab_compression(
    ("exp_group", "treatment", "control"),
    "steps",
    max_steps=8,
    assignment=assignment,
    keep=["quick_add"],
    max_events=10,
)`} path="/docs-demos/widgets/ab-compression/steps-capped.html" height={640} />

The same cap on the step screen merges neighbours too (`add_to_cart+cart`, the
checkout chain, `wishlist_add+support_chat+home`): every step cell of the
compressed screen is testable (73 of 73, 93 of 93 before), and 92.5 % of the
transitions remain. A rare event that a drop would move between two events that
never follow each other is relabelled `other` instead, so every later event
keeps its step.
